{"name":"Satan","hex":"#e63626","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Satan","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A taxonomic genus within the family Ictaluridae – widemouth blindcat."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["The supreme evil spirit in the Abrahamic religions, who tempts humanity into sin; the Devil;  the same figure, regarded as a deity to be revered and worshipped.","A person or animal regarded as particularly malignant, detestable, or evil; used as an epithet or as a name for an animal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Satan"}],"entries":[{"title":"Satan","blocks":[{"text":"Satan, also known as the Devil, is an entity in Abrahamic religions who entices humans into sin or falsehood. In Judaism, Satan is seen as an agent subservient to God, typically regarded as a metaphor for the yetzer hara, or 'evil inclination'. In Christianity and Islam, he is usually seen as a fallen angel or jinn who has rebelled against God, who nevertheless allows him temporary power over the fallen world and a host of demons."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Codex_Gigas_fol_290r_Devil.jpg/330px-Codex_Gigas_fol_290r_Devil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satan","wikidata":"Q35230"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Gustav_Jaeger_Bileam_Engel.jpg/500px-Gustav_Jaeger_Bileam_Engel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Gustav_Jaeger_Bileam_Engel.jpg/500px-Gustav_Jaeger_Bileam_Engel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Balaam and the Angel (1836) by Gustav Jäger. The angel in this incident is referred to as a \"satan\".[6]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGustav_Jaeger_Bileam_Engel.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.w